course: dictionary
lesson: 6344
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. You can tell which meaning is right by reading the words around the word. This is its context. «A baleen whale gulps a huge mouthful of sea» is about food and water going into a whale's mouth, so mouthful here means an amount that fills your mouth.
explain: Now read this: «I got a mouthful from the neighbour.» Nobody ate anything. The word got and the way it is used show that the neighbour said angry words to you. In UK slang, give someone a mouthful means to scold them, so mouthful can mean a telling-off.
explain: Some words are a mouthful because they are long and hard to say. Mastication is a long word for chewing, so it is a mouthful. Ask yourself: is this word about food, about a long name, or about someone being cross? The clues in the sentence will show you which meaning to use.
fact: Mouthful means the amount of food or drink that fills your mouth, or that you put in at one time.
fact: «A mouthful of» means the same thing as a bite or a gulp, but a mouthful is what you put in your mouth all at once.
fact: In informal speech, a mouthful is a long word or name that is hard to say, such as «mastication».
fact: In UK informal speech, «give someone a mouthful» means to say rude or angry words to them.
fact: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have chosen well.
exercise: «A baleen whale gulps a huge mouthful of sea.» What does mouthful mean here? | A rude telling-off | A long, hard word to say | A small fish | *The amount of sea that fills its mouth in one go
exercise: Mum says, «Don't talk with a mouthful of food.» What does mouthful mean? | Shouting at someone | A long, hard name | A whale's song | *Food in your mouth
exercise: Ella says, «Mastication is a mouthful.» What does she mean? | It is a rude word | *It is a long word that is hard to say | It is a big meal | A kind of fish
test: «I got a mouthful from the neighbour.» What does mouthful mean here? | The neighbour gave me a big meal | *The neighbour said angry words to me | The neighbour gave me a present
test: Which sentence uses mouthful to mean a long word that is hard to say? | «She took a mouthful of soup.» | *«Gulping: a bit of a mouthful, that name.» | «Blue whales gulp mouthfuls of krill.»
exam: «His cousin, reading over his shoulder with a mouthful of crisps, said, that's the oldest scam in the book.» What does mouthful mean here? | Crisps that are very salty | A long word that is hard to say | A telling-off from his cousin | *The crisps filling his mouth as he speaks
